  "rationale": "The prompt asks for the message reacted to by 'someone named Robert who's a member of #general', which correctly translates to a reactor-side channel-membership condition rather than requiring the message itself to reside in #general — the design's binding_note makes this explicit ('neither message needs to be in #general itself, only the reactor's membership does'), and the compiled selector implements exactly that: it joins message_reactions.user_id → channel_members.user_id → channels filtered on channel_name='general', independent of the message's own channel_id. This is a legitimate, prompt-faithful use of the CONVERSATION_MEMBERSHIP/CONVERSATION route nodes, correctly distinguishing 'reactor's channel membership' from 'message's channel' (the exact distinction the review guidance warns about), and it is now genuinely restrictive (unlike a prior iteration's non-restrictive team_name-only join) because channel_name='general' is a real filter that excludes Robert Kim.\n\nThe underspecified ambiguity is properly preserved: two #general-member Roberts (Walsh via preexisting membership, Chen via a declared seed edit) each tada-react to a distinct message, yielding candidate_sets of size one each and computed_matches exactly matching those two message IDs. The negative structure is richer and more targeted than earlier iterations, isolating three independent failure dimensions with dedicated seed_edits and reaction rows: wrong emoji from a qualifying Robert (Walsh/thumbsup), right emoji from a non-Robert (Lukas), and right name+right emoji from a Robert who fails the specific #general-membership condition (Kim, added as a new user with only #random membership) — this last negative directly and effectively exercises the CONVERSATION_MEMBERSHIP node's real semantic role. Mechanical checks report zero errors, and computed_focal_negatives/candidate_population confirm no accidental extra matches (e.g., preexisting Robert-authored messages like 1699824000.000987 and 1706067472 correctly remain unreacted-to and excluded, preserving the authorship-vs-reaction distinction).\n\nAccess is realistic: the acting bot (U01AGENBOT9) already holds memberships in #general, #engineering, #core-infra, #random, and #model-research, covering every channel needed to discover the two matches and all three negatives via conversations.members/conversations.history/reactions.get/users.info, with no reliance on workspace-name enumeration beyond the visible, non-restrictive team_name predicate. Answer-computation attributes are limited to messages.message_text, matching exactly what the prompt requests with no supplemental unrequested field. The task is a clean, well-motivated read-only retrieval with a meaningful three-way negative structure and no defects found."
}
